{"text":"Recent work supports incorporating item content into sequential recommendation, but the retrieved papers do not establish the exact combination of scratch-trained title/description encoding and a NextItNet backbone. [ConvRec (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.04723) demonstrates attribute-aware convolutional sequence modeling with hierarchical neighborhood aggregation and linear computational/memory complexity; it motivates convolutional content integration, rather than a backbone replacement. [ITS-Rec (2025)](https://www.mdpi.com/2079-9292/14/9/1748) combines titles, descriptions and reviews with sequential modeling, but uses BERT and a SASRec-derived architecture. Its strongest textual contribution comes from reviews, unavailable here, so its reported gains cannot be transferred directly.

Complementarity and substitution require particular caution. [MMSC (2025; revised 2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.22268) explicitly treats co-view/co-purchase associations as noisy, sparse supervision; its foundation-model and LLM-assisted components fall outside this experiment’s constraints. [SCRM (2024 revision)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.14957) constructs relation graphs using distinct click and purchase behaviors, then denoises them. Neither establishes that title similarity or ordinary chronological adjacency alone identifies functional compatibility, substitution or user needs.

A constraint-compatible hypothesis, inferred from these findings: retain NextItNet’s causal dilated convolutions; jointly train a small randomly initialized token-embedding/pooling encoder over available titles and descriptions; fuse its projected output with item-ID embeddings inside one network. Use the resulting representations consistently for history encoding and candidate scoring, with one checkpoint per category. Shared tokens could help sparse items, but this remains an empirical hypothesis. Tune content strength/dropout against macro validation HR@10; describe learned signals as predictive associations, not verified product relationships."}
